# Chapter 1 · What Gender Studies Is

> Before you can compare Gender Studies with anything, you must be able to say in one sentence what it is, what it studies, what it aims at, and why the world needed it. That is this chapter.

## 1.1  Defining Gender Studies

::: define Gender Studies | the working definition for the exam
Gender Studies is the multidisciplinary academic field that studies how societies construct femininity and masculinity, how these constructions shape the distribution of power, resources and opportunities between women, men and gender-diverse people, and how gender intersects with class, race, religion, age, disability and sexuality.
-> Learn this sentence. It contains the four words the examiner looks for: *constructs*, *power*, *multidisciplinary* and *intersects*.
:::

Break the definition into its parts and each part becomes a heading of an answer:

1. **An academic field.** Gender Studies has departments, degrees, journals, conferences and research methods. It is not a slogan or an NGO campaign — though it has close links with the women's movement.
2. **It studies construction.** Its starting point is that gender is made by society, not simply given by nature (Chapters 6–10).
3. **It studies power.** It asks who gains and who loses from the way gender is organised — in the family, the economy, politics, religion and law.
4. **It studies everyone.** Women, men, and people who do not fit a two-gender model; and the *relationships* between them.
5. **It is multidisciplinary.** It borrows the tools of sociology, psychology, anthropology, history, economics, political science, law, biology and literature (Chapter 3).
6. **It is intersectional.** Gender never works alone; a poor rural woman and a rich urban woman do not live the same "womanhood".

::: simple
Gender Studies asks three questions about any part of life: *What do we expect of women and of men here? Where did those expectations come from? Who benefits, who pays, and how could it be fairer?*
:::

## 1.2  What Gender Studies studies — its scope

The scope of the field is wide because gender runs through every part of social life. The main areas are:

- **Identity and the body:** how people come to see themselves as women, men or otherwise; sexuality; health and reproduction.
- **Family and household:** marriage, the division of domestic labour, care, inheritance, violence in the home.
- **Work and the economy:** paid and unpaid work, the wage gap, the informal sector, development.
- **Politics and the state:** citizenship, voting, representation, law and policy.
- **Culture and knowledge:** religion, language, media, literature, education and the way knowledge itself is produced.
- **Violence and security:** gender-based violence, conflict, peace-building.
- **Global processes:** colonialism, globalisation, migration, climate change.

::: example | One issue, many angles
Take child marriage in Pakistan. Gender Studies asks: *identity* (how girls are taught what a "good daughter" is), *family* (why parents marry daughters early — poverty, "honour", dowry), *economy* (girls' lost education and earnings), *law* (the Child Marriage Restraint Act of 1929; Sindh's law of 2013; Islamabad's law of 2025), *religion* (the debate in the Council of Islamic Ideology), *health* (early pregnancy and maternal deaths) and *power* (who decides). No single older discipline asked all these questions together.
:::

## 1.3  The aims of Gender Studies

Gender Studies has both academic and practical aims:

1. **To make the invisible visible** — to show where women (and gender) had been left out of knowledge.
2. **To explain inequality** — to find the causes of gender inequality rather than only describe it.
3. **To question "natural" arrangements** — to test whether roles that seem natural are in fact social and changeable.
4. **To produce better knowledge** — knowledge that is complete, because it includes the experience of half of humanity.
5. **To inform policy** — to give governments, courts and development agencies the evidence they need (gender statistics, gender budgeting, impact assessments).
6. **To empower** — to support the struggle for equality and dignity. This link between study and action is called **praxis**.

## 1.4  Why the discipline was needed

Gender Studies (and Women's Studies before it) was born because the existing disciplines had four blind spots. This is the answer to the 2019 question that asked you to "historically trace the need for the establishment of Gender Studies as a distinct discipline".

### (1) Knowledge was androcentric

For centuries, knowledge treated men's experience as human experience. History was the story of kings, generals and statesmen. Medicine tested drugs mostly on men and assumed women's bodies would respond the same way. Psychology built theories of moral development from studies of boys; when **Carol Gilligan** (*In a Different Voice*, ==1982==) studied girls, she found a different "ethic of care" that the standard theory had ranked as inferior.

### (2) Women's work was not counted

::: thinker Marilyn Waring | New Zealand economist and former MP · *If Women Counted* (1988)
Waring showed that the United Nations System of National Accounts, which measures GDP, counted a woman's work only when it was paid. Cooking, cleaning, caring for children and the elderly, fetching water and growing food for the family were "not work".
-> Economics had made most of women's labour invisible — so the policies built on it were blind too.
:::

### (3) The women's movement asked questions the universities could not answer

The second-wave women's movement of the 1960s (see Topic 3) asked why women earned less, why violence at home was ignored, why so few women held power. Universities had no courses, no data and no theories to answer. Women's Studies was created, in the words of its founders, as "the academic arm of the women's movement".

### (4) "Adding women" was not enough

The first step was to "add women and stir" — to add a chapter on women to a history book or a module on women to a development course. It soon became clear that this left the underlying assumptions untouched. A new field was needed that questioned the frameworks themselves, and later one that studied men and gender relations too. That field became Gender Studies (Chapter 2).

::: fig flow | Why a distinct discipline was needed: four blind spots produced a new field.
@per 5
Androcentric knowledge: man as the measure
Uncounted work: women's labour invisible
Unanswered questions: from the women's movement
"Add women and stir": not enough
Gender Studies: a field that studies gender itself
:::

## 1.5  The key features of the discipline

- **Multidisciplinary and interdisciplinary** — it uses many disciplines and increasingly blends them (Chapter 3).
- **Committed** — it openly aims at justice; critics call this a weakness (bias), supporters a strength (relevance).
- **Reflexive** — it asks who produces knowledge and from what standpoint. Feminist researchers try to do research *with* women rather than *on* them.
- **Intersectional** — since the 1980s it studies how gender combines with class, race, caste, religion and disability.
- **Comparative and global** — it compares societies to show that gender varies, and so is not fixed.

::: thinker Joan Wallach Scott | American historian · "Gender: A Useful Category of Historical Analysis" (1986)
"Gender is a constitutive element of social relationships based on perceived differences between the sexes, and gender is a primary way of signifying relationships of power."
-> Gender is not only about women; it is a way of seeing power everywhere — which is why a whole discipline is built on it.
:::

## 1.6  The significance of Gender Studies — especially for Pakistan

::: pakistan | Why Pakistan needs the discipline
- **Policy:** Pakistan reports to the UN under CEDAW (acceded ==1996==) and has committed to SDG 5 (gender equality by ==2030==). Both need gender data, analysis and trained officials.
- **Development:** Pakistan ranked ==143rd of 145== in the Global Gender Gap Report 2026. Closing that gap needs research on why women's labour-force participation (==24.4%==, LFS 2024–25) and literacy (about ==53%==, Census 2023) are so low.
- **Law:** Parliament and provincial assemblies have passed many pro-women laws since 2004; Gender Studies graduates staff the commissions, protection centres and NGOs that implement them.
- **Governance:** gender budgeting and gender-responsive planning depend on the skills the field teaches.
- **Social change:** the field gives society a language — "gender", "harassment", "consent", "care work" — to discuss problems once treated as private.
:::

::: eye | The one-paragraph "significance" answer
When a question asks for "scope and significance", give one heading to each: *academic* significance (complete knowledge; new methods), *policy* significance (CEDAW, SDGs, gender budgeting), *social* significance (awareness, changing norms) and *Pakistani* significance (data, law, institutions). Four headings, each with one piece of evidence, is a full answer to that limb.
:::
